# 0003-trusted-proxy-rightmost.patch — resolve the client IP from the RIGHT of
# the X-Forwarded-For chain.
#
# Files: src/interfaces/middleware/trusted_proxy.rs
#        src/interfaces/middleware/trace_span.rs      (comment only)
#        docs/architecture/magic-link-auth.md         (doc: "leftmost" claim)
#        docs/config/deployment.md                    (doc: the operator rule)
# Applies to: a clean tree at HEAD (389e161b). Standalone: disjoint from
#             0001/0002/0004.
# Policy flag: none — but it changes the rate-limit KEY for every deployment
# behind a trusted proxy, so it is the patch most worth a deliberate look.
#
# WHAT IT CHANGES (behaviour)
#   1. With a trusted peer, the effective client IP is the RIGHTMOST
#      `X-Forwarded-For` entry that parses as an IP and is not itself inside a
#      trusted CIDR. Before: the LEFTMOST entry — a value the client supplies,
#      because proxies append (`$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for`). Effect before: any
#      client chose its own rate-limit bucket (limiter bypass) and could spend a
#      victim's budget by naming the victim's address.
#   2. Entries are parsed and compared normalised (`::ffff:203.0.113.9` ==
#      `203.0.113.9`) and rendered canonically (`203.0.113.9`, `2001:db8::1`), so
#      two spellings of one address are one bucket. Unparseable entries are
#      skipped, not fatal (junk can only appear in the attacker-supplied region).
#   3. `X-Real-Ip` stays the fallback under the same rule (used when it is not
#      itself a trusted proxy); if neither header identifies a client the TCP
#      peer is used — including for an all-trusted chain, which is the
#      "cannot tell clients apart" case and therefore one shared bucket.
#   4. `ClientIpDisplay` no longer borrows from the request: the `Forwarded(&str)`
#      variant becomes `ForwardedIp(IpAddr)` and the lifetime parameter is gone,
#      so the type owns everything it renders while still allocating nothing.
#      Rendering is byte-identical to `client_ip_from_parts` (asserted).
#   5. Docs: the module header, the `magic-link-auth.md` "leftmost" sentence and
#      the `OXICLOUD_TRUST_PROXY_CIDR` note in `deployment.md` all describe the
#      new rule; the deployment note now tells operators to APPEND, not replace.
#
# WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CHANGE
#   * `client_ip` / `client_ip_from_parts` keep their signatures and still return
#     a bare IP string (no port, no brackets) for `include_port = false` — the
#     rate-limit path (`rate_limit::extract_client_ip`) is unchanged.
#   * An entry carrying a port (`203.0.113.9:443`, i.e. a proxy configured with
#     `$remote_addr:$remote_port`) is treated as malformed and skipped, so a
#     chain that consists of such entries resolves to the peer while a
#     `junk, <spoofed>`-style chain can still resolve into the attacker-supplied
#     region. Pinned as a known limitation in the test rather than papered over:
#     `$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for` is the documented configuration.
#   * `middleware/dpop.rs` also reads the LEFTMOST `X-Forwarded-Proto/Host` — a
#     different mechanism with a different failure mode (it reconstructs `htu`,
#     not a client identity) and a separate decision; untouched.
#   * `OXICLOUD_TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=1` (deprecated trust-all) now yields the peer
#     for every request, i.e. one global bucket, since every hop is "inside" the
#     trust-all CIDRs. Documented in the module header; not special-cased.
#   * `docs/re/behavioral-model.md` / `docs/re/experiment-matrix.md` keep
#     describing the v0.10.0 binary's leftmost behaviour: those are baseline RE
#     artifacts, not product docs, and 0003 is what makes them stale-on-purpose.
#
# TESTS THAT PIN IT (trusted_proxy.rs #[cfg(test)])
#   Both live in a re-exec'd child with `OXICLOUD_TRUST_PROXY_CIDR=127.0.0.1/32,
